---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const energyCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '0.5*3*v^2/1000', label: 'kinetic energy (kJ), 3 kg', color: '#ea580c', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '3*9.81*x/1000', label: 'potential energy (kJ), 3 kg', color: '#0f766e', width: 2.0 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'speed v (m/s) — or height x (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1.5, label: 'energy (kJ)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 330,
};

const foundationsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Newton\'s second law', note: 'F = ma. The whole of dynamics is this equation applied honestly: find the forces, find the motion.' },
    { math: '2 · Work and energy', note: 'Work = force × distance. Integrating F = ma over distance gives the work–energy theorem: net work equals the change in kinetic energy.' },
    { math: '3 · Conservation when forces are friendly', note: 'Gravity and springs store work reversibly, so kinetic + potential energy stays constant. Friction is the tax collector.' },
    { math: '4 · Momentum for collisions', note: 'Impulse = force × time. Over a collision, momentum is conserved even when energy is not — that is why crumple zones work.' },
    { math: '5 · Rigid bodies add rotation', note: 'The same laws, rotated: torque replaces force, moment of inertia replaces mass, ω replaces v. Every machine is this chapter.' },
  ],
};

const mechAngleMatch = {
  "prompt": "One law, three useful angles — pair each with what it does:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "work–energy",
      "right": "integrate over distance — answers \"how fast?\" without caring about the path"
    },
    {
      "left": "impulse–momentum",
      "right": "integrate over time — collision questions without the enormous forces inside the impact"
    },
    {
      "left": "rotation",
      "right": "turn the whole apparatus sideways — spinning shafts as cleanly as sliding blocks"
    }
  ],
  "why": "They are not different laws: each is Newton's second law re-packaged — by distance, by time, or by axis."
};

const mechFbdOrder = {
  "prompt": "The free-body ritual, in order — drag the steps into place:",
  "items": [
    "Isolate the one body you care about",
    "Draw every external force acting on it as an arrow",
    "Hunt the gaps: a missing support or a forgotten friction arrow shows on paper",
    "Only now write the equations"
  ],
  "why": "A gap on paper is visible; a gap inside algebra is invisible — that is the whole point of drawing first."
};

const mechSledgePlace = {
  "prompt": "The gardener's 200 N pull is held 30° above the horizontal. Place the marker on the vertical share doing the lifting:",
  "labels": [
    "0",
    "50",
    "100",
    "150",
    "200"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 200
  },
  "answer": 100,
  "tolerance": 4,
  "why": "200 × sin 30° = 100 N straight up — it lightens the sledge from 392 N to about 292 N pressing on the ground, and the friction falls with it."
};

const mechThirdLawSpot = {
  "prompt": "A note on Newton's third law — one line is wrong. Click it:",
  "lines": [
    "A third-law pair is equal in size and opposite in direction.",
    "The two members act on different bodies — one on you, one on the ground.",
    "Because they are equal and opposite, they cancel and nothing can accelerate.",
    "A sprinter accelerates because only one member of the pair appears in the sprinter's own diagram.",
    "The matching force belongs to the Earth's bookkeeping, and the Earth barely notices."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Cancellation requires both forces to act on the same body; a third-law pair never meets that condition, so the sprinter is free to accelerate.",
  "why": "Action and reaction are a bookkeeping pair across two bodies, not a balance on one."
};

const mechFallJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Without air resistance, every mass accelerates under gravity at the same 9.81 m/s².",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Mass appears on both sides of F = m·a and cancels."
    },
    {
      "s": "Gravity pulls harder, per kilogram, on the heavier object.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Per kilogram the pull is identical — weight and inertia grow together and cancel."
    },
    {
      "s": "A bowling ball beats a falling leaf only because air drag robs the leaf sooner relative to its weight.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The race is settled by drag versus weight, not by gravity picking favourites."
    },
    {
      "s": "Doubling a body's mass doubles its free-fall acceleration.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Free-fall acceleration stays 9.81 m/s² whatever the mass."
    }
  ]
};

const mechKinCloze = {
  "text": "For steady acceleration, %%0%% grows in proportion to time (v = v₀ + a·t), while distance grows with the %%1%% of time — one reason a car at twice the speed needs far more than twice the road to stop.",
  "answers": [
    "speed",
    "square"
  ],
  "hint": "The same squaring that sets braking distances lives in the second blank."
};

const mechTipEstimate = {
  "prompt": "A large wind-turbine blade turns slowly and serenely at the hub. Roughly what speed can its tip reach in strong winds?",
  "unit": "km/h",
  "answer": 250,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "v = ω·r — modest angular speed times a very large radius: the tip passes 250 km/h while the hub barely seems to move.",
  "context": "Same law at small scale: the rim of a spinning disc howls while its middle is silent."
};

const mechInertiaTrace = {
  "prompt": "A fixed mass moved farther from the axis — which curve is its moment of inertia?",
  "curves": [
    {
      "expr": "x^2",
      "color": "#1d4ed8",
      "label": "A"
    },
    {
      "expr": "2*x",
      "color": "#b42318",
      "label": "B"
    },
    {
      "expr": "x^0.5",
      "color": "#0f766e",
      "label": "C"
    }
  ],
  "correct": 0,
  "x": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 4
  },
  "y": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 17
  },
  "xlabel": "distance from axis (arb. units)",
  "ylabel": "moment of inertia (arb. units)",
  "why": "I grows with the square of distance: doubling the mass doubles I, but the same mass at twice the radius quadruples it — which is why light rims can out-muscle a heavy frame."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/mechanical-engineering/foundational/mechanics-foundations">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Mechanical Engineering. You need nothing from other
    engineering lessons — only everyday arithmetic and a willingness to think slowly. A
    little trigonometry appears in one worked example; it is explained on the spot, and you
    can follow every line without having met it before.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what fully specifies a force — magnitude, direction and point of application — and why engineers draw a free-body diagram before touching any algebra;</li>
    <li>resolve a slanted pull into horizontal and vertical parts and predict what each part does;</li>
    <li>describe motion with position, velocity and acceleration, and with their rotational twins — angle, angular speed, angular acceleration;</li>
    <li>apply Newton's second law in straight-line and rotating form: <code>F = m·a</code> and <code>τ = I·α</code>;</li>
    <li>use the work–energy theorem to answer "how fast?" questions without tracing the path;</li>
    <li>use impulse–momentum to handle collisions, crumple zones and bat-and-ball impacts;</li>
    <li>explain how a gearbox, a flywheel, a brake and a crumple zone are all the same law wearing different clothes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the whole lesson: before any formula, ask what it is keeping
    track of. The mathematics of this subject is short; the discipline is in knowing what
    each symbol stands for.
  </p>

  <h2>One law, three angles</h2>
  <p>
    Push a stalled car and it speeds up. Brake, and it slows. Hit a kerb and something
    bends. Every one of those everyday outcomes is the same single law at work, and
    mechanical engineering is that law put to use: bodies, forces and motion, with heat and
    materials added later. The subject's foundations fit on a postcard — force equals mass
    times acceleration, work equals force times distance, impulse equals force times time —
    and they are deep enough that gearboxes, cranes, engine cranks and wind turbines are all
    consequences of those few lines. The subject is three centuries old and has not needed a
    correction inside everyday engineering: when a mechanism behaves differently from the
    prediction, the laws are not wrong, the force inventory was.
  </p>
  <p>
    What separates a competent engineer from a formula-matcher is knowing that the postcard
    lines are not really different laws. They are one law — Newton's second law — seen from
    three useful angles. Integrate it over <em>distance</em> and you get the work–energy
    theorem, which answers "how fast?" without caring about the path taken. Integrate it
    over <em>time</em> and you get impulse–momentum, which answers collision questions
    without needing to know the enormous forces inside the impact. Rotate the whole
    apparatus and it describes spinning shafts as cleanly as sliding blocks. Much of
    engineering analysis is simply choosing the angle that turns a ten-line problem into a
    one-line problem.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each angle with the integration that buys it" widget="match" config={mechAngleMatch} />
  <p>
    The chain below previews the whole lesson in five steps. Read it now, then again when
    you reach the end — the second reading should feel completely different.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain from F = ma to machines" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each step re-packages the one before it; there is no
    new physics after step 1. Step 2 is step 1 multiplied by distance. Step 3 is what step
    2 says when the forces store energy and give it back instead of wasting it. Step 4 is
    step 1 multiplied by time. Step 5 is the whole apparatus turned sideways for rotation.
    That is why one lesson can honestly cover brakes, roller coasters, crumple zones and
    gearboxes at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the whole subject hangs on a single law. The rest of the
    lesson builds its three angles in order — first forces as honest vectors, then motion
    and its causes, then the two shortcuts that make hard problems easy.
  </p>

  <h2>Vector mechanics: forces with directions</h2>
  <p>
    A force is not a number. It is fully specified only by three things: its
    <strong>magnitude</strong> (its size, measured in newtons — 1 N is roughly the weight
    of a small apple), its <strong>direction</strong>, and its <strong>point of
    application</strong> (where on the body it acts). Forget any of the three and the
    answer comes out exactly, confidently wrong. <strong>Vector mechanics</strong> is the
    discipline of keeping those directions honest.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tool that enforces the honesty is the <strong>free-body diagram</strong>: isolate
    the one body you care about, draw every external force acting <em>on</em> it as an
    arrow, and only then write equations. The diagram can look like a formality, but it is
    where mistakes are caught — a missing support force or a forgotten friction arrow is
    visible as a gap on paper, and invisible inside algebra.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="rebuild the free-body procedure" widget="order" config={mechFbdOrder} />
  <p>
    Forces acting at a slant are handled by resolving them into components. A worked
    example: a gardener drags a 40 kg loaded sledge with a rope held at 30° above the
    horizontal, tension 200 N. The pull splits into a horizontal share of 200 × cos 30° ≈
    173 N (the cosine of an angle is the fraction of the pull that acts along the ground)
    and a vertical share of 200 × sin 30° = 100 N that lifts <em>upward</em>. That upward
    share matters: it lightens the sledge from a weight of 40 × 9.81 ≈ 392 N to about
    292 N pressing on the ground, and friction — which here runs at about 0.4 of the
    pressing force — drops from roughly 157 N to 0.4 × 292 ≈ 117 N. The net forward pull
    is therefore 173 − 117 ≈ 56 N, accelerating the sledge at 56 ÷ 40 ≈ 1.4 m/s². One
    trigonometric decomposition turned a muddy real situation into three clean numbers, and
    revealed a subtlety pure intuition misses: pulling <em>up</em> at an angle reduces
    friction.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="drop the marker on the lifting share" widget="place" config={mechSledgePlace} />
  <p>
    Vectors add a second idea: the <strong>moment</strong> — the turning effect of a force,
    its size times the perpendicular distance from the pivot. Moments explain why a long
    spanner loosens a bolt a short one cannot, why doorknobs live far from hinges, and why
    a crane's counterweight sits far from the tower: distance buys as much leverage as
    weight. Equilibrium of a rigid body in two dimensions is exactly three equations —
    horizontal forces sum to zero, vertical forces sum to zero, moments about any point sum
    to zero — and every statics problem in the discipline is a disguise for those three.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more piece of honesty: forces come in pairs. Newton's third law says that if body A
    pushes body B, body B pushes back on A equally and oppositely. Your feet press the
    ground and the ground presses your feet; walking is possible only because the Earth is
    included in the accounting.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "action and reaction cancel, so nothing should ever
  accelerate." The two forces of a third-law pair act on <em>different</em> bodies — the
  ground's push acts on you, your push acts on the ground — so they can never cancel on the
  same body. A sprinter accelerates because the push <em>on the sprinter</em> is the only
  member of the pair that appears in the sprinter's free-body diagram; the matching force
  belongs to the Earth's bookkeeping, and the Earth, being vastly heavier, barely
  notices.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="one line of the third-law note cancels wrongly" widget="spot-error" config={mechThirdLawSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "heavy objects fall faster." Without air resistance,
  every mass accelerates under gravity at the same 9.81 m/s² — mass appears on both sides
  of <code>F = m·a</code> and cancels. A bowling ball does beat a feather to the ground,
  but only because air drag robs the feather sooner relative to its weight, not because
  gravity pulls harder per kilogram. On the Moon, where an Apollo astronaut dropped a
  hammer and a feather side by side, they landed together.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — who falls faster?" widget="judge" config={mechFallJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> forces are arrows with three attributes, collected on
    free-body diagrams; slant is handled by components, turning effects by moments, and
    equilibrium is three equations. That machinery answers "will it move, and how fast?"
    Next, motion itself gets its own vocabulary.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinematics and kinetics of rigid bodies</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Kinematics</em> describes motion without asking what causes it. In a straight line
    the cast is position, velocity and acceleration, and for steady acceleration two short
    equations do most of the work: <code>v = v₀ + a·t</code> (speed grows in proportion to
    time) and <code>s = v₀·t + ½·a·t²</code> (distance grows with the <em>square</em> of
    time — one reason a car at twice the speed needs far more than twice the road to stop).
    For rotation the story repeats term for term: angle θ replaces position, angular
    velocity ω replaces speed, angular acceleration α replaces acceleration, and every
    linear equation has a rotational twin.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — the kinematics that sets braking" widget="cloze" config={mechKinCloze} />
  <p>
    The bridge between the two worlds is <code>v = ω·r</code>: a point on a spinning body
    at radius r moves with speed equal to angular velocity times radius. It is why the
    outer edge of a grinding wheel screams while its centre is silent, and why a large
    wind-turbine blade, turning slowly and serenely at the hub, can push its tip past
    250 km/h in strong winds.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the tip speed" widget="estimate" config={mechTipEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Kinetics</em> supplies the cause: Newton's second law in rotational dress. Torque τ
    (a moment, measured in newton-metres) plays the role of force, and the
    <strong>moment of inertia</strong> I plays the role of mass. I measures not just how
    much matter a body has but how far that matter sits from the axis, in units of kg·m².
    The law reads <code>τ = I·α</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: a flywheel of moment of inertia 2 kg·m² driven by a net torque of
    6 N·m spins up at α = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 rad/s², so from rest it reaches 150 rad/s (about
    1,430 rpm) in 50 seconds — and by then it stores ½·I·ω² = 0.5 × 2 × 150² = 22.5 kJ of
    rotational energy. That is a one-line calculation, and sizing a real energy-storage
    flywheel extends it directly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a heavier wheel is always harder to spin up." Mass
  alone does not set the difficulty — distribution does, because moment of inertia grows
  with the <em>square</em> of distance from the axis. Doubling a flywheel's mass doubles
  its inertia; moving the same mass twice as far from the axis quadruples it. This is why
  a light bicycle wheel with heavy rims can feel harder to accelerate than a heavier
  frame, and why flywheel designers push mass outward on purpose.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="which curve is the inertia law?" widget="trace" config={mechInertiaTrace} />
  <p>
    Rotation is also where machines make their living. Gears are the promise "trade torque
    for speed" made hardware: mesh a 20-tooth pinion into a 60-tooth wheel and the wheel
    turns at one third the speed with three times the torque — the gear ratio — losing only
    a little to friction. A car's gearbox is nothing but a switchable set of such ratios,
    keeping the engine near its efficient speed while the wheels cover everything from
    parking to highway.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> kinematics describes motion, kinetics causes it, and rotation
    mirrors straight-line motion symbol for symbol through <code>v = ω·r</code> and
    <code>τ = I·α</code>. The next section adds a shortcut powerful enough to replace both
    when the question is "how fast?" rather than "what happens next?".
  </p>

  <h2>Work–energy: the power of not caring about the path</h2>
  <p>
    Newton's law answers "what happens next, instant by instant" — powerful, but laborious
    when forces vary along the way. Multiply force by the <em>distance</em> moved along the
    force and you get <strong>work</strong>, measured in joules; the work–energy theorem
    says the net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy, <code>½·m·v²</code>.
    The magic is that the path disappears: know only the start and end states, and the
    theorem hands you the answer with no intermediate steps.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most safety-critical application is braking. A tyre gripping dry asphalt
    decelerates a car at roughly 0.7 × 9.81 ≈ 6.9 m/s². Skidding to a stop from 20 m/s
    takes v² ÷ (2 × 6.9) ≈ 29 m. From 40 m/s the distance is about 117 m — four times, not
    twice, because kinetic energy grows with the <em>square</em> of speed. The same
    squaring sets crash severity: a 1,500 kg car at 50 km/h (about 13.9 m/s) carries
    ½ × 1500 × 13.9² ≈ 145 kJ, and something must absorb all of it before the occupants
    stop moving.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plot below shows the theorem's two stores side by side: kinetic energy for a 3 kg
    body as speed grows, and gravitational potential energy — energy stored by lifting,
    <code>m·g·h</code> — as height grows, on one shared pair of axes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="KE grows with v², PE with h — drag and compare" widget="plotter" config={energyCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the potential-energy line is straight — double the
    height, double the stored energy. The kinetic-energy curve bends upward — double the
    speed and the energy <em>quadruples</em>. Follow both to the right edge: at 30 m/s the
    3 kg body carries 1.35 kJ, more than lifting the same body 30 m (about 0.88 kJ). Speed
    is the expensive commodity, which is the entire content of the braking numbers above.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the only forces doing work are gravity and springs — <strong>conservative</strong>
    forces, which give back exactly what they store — kinetic plus potential energy is
    conserved outright, and problems become comparisons of two bookkeeping entries. A
    roller-coaster car released from 40 m can reach, at best, <code>√(2·g·h)</code> =
    √(2 × 9.81 × 40) ≈ 28 m/s at the bottom; friction takes its tax from whatever remains.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "momentum and kinetic energy are interchangeable."
  They are not — momentum is <code>m·v</code> and is a vector; kinetic energy is
  <code>½·m·v²</code> and is a scalar. In a collision, momentum is essentially always
  conserved; kinetic energy is conserved only in ideal elastic impacts. Two lumps of clay
  that stick together conserve momentum exactly while converting most of their kinetic
  energy into heat and deformation — both statements are true simultaneously, and
  engineering crash analysis depends on holding both at once.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the work–energy theorem buys freedom from the path, at the
    price of tracking stored energy instead of forces. For collisions, where the forces are
    violent and unknown, a different integration pays better.
  </p>

  <h2>Impulse–momentum: mastering the collision</h2>
  <p>
    Multiply force by the <em>time</em> it acts and you get <strong>impulse</strong>, and
    impulse equals the change in <strong>momentum</strong> (mass × velocity): force ×
    duration = <code>m·Δv</code>. This is the right tool whenever huge forces act for short
    times — impacts, propulsion, ball games — because you rarely know the peak force, but
    you can often know how long it acted.
  </p>
  <p>
    A crumple zone is impulse management made metal. The car must lose 13.9 m/s of velocity
    no matter what, so the design question is how much <em>time and distance</em> to spend
    losing it. Crush 0.5 m of structure at 50 km/h and the average deceleration is
    v² ÷ (2 × 0.5) ≈ 193 m/s², about 20 g; double the crush distance and the deceleration
    halves. Every safety feature in a modern car — crumple zones, airbags, helmet foam — is
    an argument about stretching one fixed change of velocity over more time.
  </p>
  <p>
    In collisions between free bodies, total momentum is conserved even as energy turns
    into heat and sound, which lets engineers compute post-impact motion from pre-impact
    velocities alone. That single fact underlies accident reconstruction (from skid marks
    and final positions back to impact speeds), ballistics, and the docking of spacecraft,
    where a soft capture is defined as one that conserves momentum while dissipating the
    relative speed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sport supplies the friendliest numbers. A pitched baseball arrives at 40 m/s and leaves
    the bat at 45 m/s the other way; the 0.145 kg ball's momentum change is 0.145 ×
    (40 + 45) ≈ 12.3 kg·m/s. Bat and ball are in contact for about a millisecond, so the
    average force during contact is 12.3 ÷ 0.001 ≈ 12,000 N — the weight of a small car,
    applied for one thousandth of a second. Nobody feels a force of that size as such; what
    the hitter feels is the impulse. Follow-through matters in every bat-and-ball sport for
    exactly this reason: it lengthens the contact time, buying more momentum change for the
    same peak force.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What three things fully specify a force, and why does an engineer draw a free-body diagram before writing any equation?</li>
    <li>A car can brake from 40 km/h in 12 m. Roughly what braking distance does the same car need from 80 km/h, and why?</li>
    <li>A 20-tooth pinion drives a 60-tooth wheel. What happens to speed and torque at the wheel?</li>
    <li>Two lumps of clay collide and stick. Which is conserved — momentum, kinetic energy, or both?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Magnitude, direction and point of application; the
    diagram lays out every external force on the body so none is silently missed — algebra
    built on an incomplete inventory is exactly, confidently wrong. (2) About 48 m — four
    times as far: kinetic energy grows with the square of speed, and the brakes must remove
    all of it within the stopping distance. (3) The wheel turns at one third the speed with
    three times the torque — the gear ratio trades speed for turning effort, and the power
    delivered is nearly unchanged. (4) Momentum only. It is conserved for the pair in every
    collision; the kinetic energy mostly converts to heat and deformation, because sticking
    is about as far from an elastic impact as collisions get.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A force has magnitude, direction and point of application; the free-body diagram is the honesty check that precedes all algebra.</li>
    <li>Slant forces are handled by components, turning effects by moments, and equilibrium is three equations in two dimensions.</li>
    <li>Newton's third-law pairs act on different bodies, so they never cancel on the same one.</li>
    <li>Kinematics describes motion; kinetics causes it. Rotation mirrors the linear story through <code>v = ω·r</code> and <code>τ = I·α</code>.</li>
    <li>Moment of inertia depends on where mass sits, not just how much there is — distance counts squared.</li>
    <li>Work–energy (force × distance) answers "how fast?" without the path; kinetic energy grows with v², so braking and crash severity grow with it.</li>
    <li>Impulse–momentum (force × time) masters collisions: momentum is conserved, kinetic energy usually is not.</li>
    <li>Gears trade torque for speed; crumple zones trade distance for gentler deceleration — one law, different bargains.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson asks whether parts survive the forces these laws demand: stress,
    fatigue and failure, then the machine elements — shafts, bearings, gears — that package
    this theory into hardware. Thermodynamics follows, supplying the accounting for the
    heat that friction and collisions create here as an afterthought. Nothing in those
    lessons will overturn a line of this one; they build on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Rigid-body dynamics keeps a second conservation law in reserve: <strong>angular
    momentum</strong>, <code>I·ω</code>, is conserved when no external torque acts. It
    explains the figure skater who pulls in her arms — I falls, so ω rises to keep the
    product fixed — and the reaction wheels that reorient spacecraft without firing
    thrusters. The work–energy theorem also extends to rolling bodies, whose kinetic energy
    splits between translation and rotation (<code>½·m·v² + ½·I·ω²</code>): a solid disc
    stores less of its energy in spin than a hoop of the same mass and radius, which is why
    the disc wins a race down the same slope. And when inertia must be taken about an axis
    that misses the centre of mass, the parallel-axis theorem adjusts it:
    <code>I = I<sub>cm</sub> + m·d²</code>, where d is the offset — spinning about a
    far-away axis is always harder than spinning about the centre.
  </p>
  <p>
    Further depth moves into machinery and vibration. D'Alembert's trick treats
    mass-times-acceleration as an inertial force, converting dynamics problems into statics
    ones — the conceptual basis of load cases in vehicle and crane design. Virtual work
    generalises equilibrium to systems of linked bodies, and energy methods grow into
    Lagrange's equations, the standard tool for multi-part mechanisms. Vibration enters
    when stiffness joins inertia: a spring–mass system has a natural frequency
    <code>f = (1 ÷ 2π)·√(k ÷ m)</code>, and rotating machinery is designed so its operating
    speed never dwells near that number — passing through it quickly at startup, parking on
    it never. The open frontier is less the laws than the bookkeeping at scale: multibody
    simulation of hundred-part mechanisms, contact and friction models that stay stable,
    and digital twins that keep a Newtonian model synchronised with a real machine wearing
    out in the field.
  </p>

